-->

    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-2 text-center">
        <div
            class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3 pt-6 pb-4"
        >
            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.getStarted.url}
                class="btn btn-primary"
            >
                Get Started
            </a>

            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.navMenu.learnYini.quickTutorial.url}
                class="inline-block underline"
            >
                Quick tutorial
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-20 pb-4">
        <h2>🌐 The YINI Ecosystem</h2>

        <p class="mb-4 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
            A high-level overview of the main YINI specification, tooling,
            implementation, and adoption pieces.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700">
                        <th class="py-3 text-left font-semibold">🧠 Core</th>
                        <th class="py-3 text-left font-semibold"
                            >🛠 Implementation</th
                        >
                        <th class="py-3 text-left font-semibold">🌍 Adoption</th
                        >
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <tbody>
                    {
                        rows.map((row) => (
                            <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 transition hover:bg-slate-50 dark:border-slate-800 dark:hover:bg-slate-900">
                                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                                    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                                        <span class="text-green-600">
                                            {statusIcon(row.core.status)}
                                        </span>
                                        {row.core.label}
                                    </div>
                                </td>

                                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                                    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                                        <span
                                            class={
                                                row.impl.status === 'done'
                                                    ? 'text-green-600'
                                                    : row.impl.status ===
                                                        'inprogress'
                                                      ? 'text-yellow-500'
                                                      : 'text-slate-400'
                                            }>
                                            {statusIcon(row.impl.status)}
                                        </span>
                                        {row.impl.label}
                                    </div>
                                </td>

                                <td class="py-3">
                                    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                                        <span
                                            class={
                                                row.adopt.status === 'done'
                                                    ? 'text-green-600'
                                                    : row.adopt.status ===
                                                        'inprogress'
                                                      ? 'text-yellow-500'
                                                      : 'text-slate-400'
                                            }>
                                            {statusIcon(row.adopt.status)}
                                        </span>
                                        {row.adopt.label}
                                    </div>
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        ))
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div
            class="mt-4 text-center text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400"
        >
            ✔ Complete &nbsp; ◐ In progress &nbsp; ○ Planned
        </div>
        <div
            class="mt-4 text-center text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400"
        >
            RC &nbsp;=&nbsp; Release Candidate
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-20 pb-4">
        <h2>Who is YINI for?</h2>
        <p>YINI configuration files are a good fit for:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Local or internal settings/config files where you want a clear,
                predictable file that people can understand and hand-edit
                safely.
            </li>
            <li>
                Developers who want readable, structured configuration without
                relying on indentation-sensitive syntax.
            </li>
            <li>
                Teams that want comments, useful data types, and nested sections
                without JSON workarounds or flat INI-style sections.
            </li>
            <li>
                Projects that benefit from predictable parsing in tools, CI, and
                application configuration.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            YINI is early-stage: tooling is growing, community is small —
            However the specification is in release-candidate state, and the
            official parsers, CLI, and test suite are suitable to try in
            projects where you control the stack.
        </p>

        <h3>Real YINI configuration used by this site</h3>

        <p>
            This site uses <code>site-config.yini</code> as a real YINI configuration
            file in the <code>yini-homepage</code> repository.
        </p>

        <p>
            During the build workflow, <a href="/tools/yini-cli"
                ><code>yini-cli</code></a
            > converts it to JSON:
        </p>

        <pre><code class="language-bash">npx yini-cli@latest parse site-config.yini --output src/config/site-config.json</code></pre>

        <p>
            This generates <code>src/config/site-config.json</code>, which is
            read by
            <code>config.ts</code> and used by this Astro site.
        </p>

        <p>
            Repository:
            <a
                href="https://github.com/YINI-lang/yini-homepage"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
                github.com/YINI-lang/yini-homepage
            </a>
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-6">
        <h2>📝 Example</h2>
        <h3>YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="./samples/settings.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/90"
                    src="./samples/settings.yini.png"
                    alt="File settings.yini"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a href="./samples/settings.yini" target="_blank"
                >settings.yini</a
            >
        </p>

        <h3>Parsed output (JSON):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "Settings": {
        "serviceId": "NebulaService",
        "release": "3.2.1",
        "debugMode": false,
        "tagline": "\"Nebula\" is a cloud of gas and dust in outer space.",
        "Network": {
            "bindAddress": "127.0.0.1",
            "bindPort": 8080,
            "allowedOrigins": [
                "https://myapp.com",
                "http://localhost:3000"
            ]
        },
        "Capabilities": {
            "enableSearch": true,
            "experimental": [
                "new-ui",
                "streaming-api"
            ],
            "pools": {
                "min_units": 2,
                "max_units": 5,
                "size_mb": 128,
                "timeout_sec": 90
            }
        },
        "DB Config": {
            "host": "db.internal",
            "ssl": true,
            "Security": {
                "username": "service_user",
                "password": "****"
            }
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
            New to YINI? Read a short <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.navMenu.learnYini.learnYiniFormat.url}
                class="underline">learn the YINI format</a
            >.
        </p>
    </section>

    <div class="yini-features full-bleed px-8">
        <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl pt-1 pb-6">
            <h2>🧩 Key Features</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <strong>Clear section markers</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
                        Sections start with <code>^</code> by default:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code>^ App
title = "MyApp"</code></pre>
                    <p>
                        Nested sections use repeated <code>^</code> markers to indicate
                        section levels — similar concept like Markdown's heading
                        levels:
                        <code>^ App</code>, <code>^^ Server</code>, <code
                            >^^^ Logging</code
                        >. This creates a clear section hierarchy without
                        relying on indentation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        YINI also supports alternative section markers such as <code
                            >&lt;</code
                        >.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Predictable and well-defined</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Defined by a formal, versioned specification and grammar
                        (<code>ANTLR4</code>) to ensure consistent behavior
                        across tools.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Simple key–value pairs</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
                        Inside a section, configuration is written as:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code>key = value</code></pre>
                    <p>
                        This keeps files readable and familiar, inspired by
                        classic INI.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Structured like JSON, readable like INI</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
                        Useful data types (similar to JSON):
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Nested sections</li>
                        <li>Lists (arrays)</li>
                        <li>Maps / Objects (JS like objects)</li>
                        <li>Strings and numbers</li>
                        <li>Booleans and nulls</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Comments where you expect them</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
                        Supports both line comments (<code>#</code>, <code
                            >//</code
                        >, <code>;</code> and block/multi-line comments <code
                            >/* ... */</code
                        >), making it easy to document configuration directly in
                        the file.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Reduced syntax noise</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
                        Keeps files readable by avoiding excessive brackets,
                        quotes, and long dotted key paths such as <code
                            >server.database.advanced.param</code
                        >.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Free and open source</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        YINI is open-source software, free to use, inspect, and
                        build upon.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                💡 For syntax & quick reference: See <a
                    href={CONFIG.siteLinks.navMenu.refs.cheatSheet.url}
                    class="inline-block underline"
                >
                    YINI Cheat Sheet
                </a>
            </p>
        </section>
    </div>

    <section class="my-6 pt-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">⚖️ How YINI compares to other config formats</h2>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto">
            <p class="mb-2 text-sm">
                This is a high-level comparison focused on configuration-file
                use cases.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
                YINI supports both lenient and strict parsing modes, depending
                on how much enforcement you need. Lenient mode is the default.
            </p>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>YINI (lenient)</th>
                        <th>YINI (strict)</th>
                        <th>INI</th>
                        <th>JSON</th>
                        <th>YAML</th>
                        <th>TOML</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Human readability by default</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Readability at scale</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
